# [M6.4.3] D27 blinded corpus -- atomic-groups module.
#
# Basic (?>X) semantics: X matches at the current position on X's OWN first
# attempt, and the group then refuses to reconsider that attempt even if
# what follows fails. Every case here was measured against libpcre2 10.46
# via docs/design/eng_brep_measurements/probes/pcre2_ctypes.py at options=0
# (any (?i)/(?m) is inline, part of the pattern text). See README.md for
# the oracle-verification command and the full author's note.
#
# `atomic-groups` is not in pcrec's default feature set (PCREC_DEFAULT_
# FEATURES = "std1" = {classes, modifiers}), so every block below carries
# its own `features atomic-groups` directive -- omitting it would make
# every case here fail on the module gate alone, once the module exists,
# regardless of whether the semantics below are implemented correctly.

# A plain atomic group is just a group: it matches, or it doesn't.
pattern (?>ab)
features atomic-groups
m "ab" 0 2
n "ac"

pattern (?>ab)c
features atomic-groups
m "abc" 0 3

# Alternation PRIORITY inside an atomic group: the first alternative that
# matches wins and is committed to, even though a later alternative would
# also have matched (here, matching more).
pattern (?>a|ab)
features atomic-groups
m "ab" 0 1

# The classic atomic-alternation trap: committing to the shorter "a" loses
# the "c" that would follow "ab" -- and there is no retry to try "ab"
# instead, so the whole group (and hence the whole match attempt at this
# start position, and every other start position in "abc") fails.
pattern (?>a|ab)c
features atomic-groups
n "abc"
m "ac" 0 2

# Reordering the alternatives changes which one is committed to, and here
# that changes the outcome versus the case above: matching "ab" first
# leaves "c" available afterward.
pattern (?>ab|a)c
features atomic-groups
m "abc" 0 3

# a+ eats every 'a' available on its own first (greedy) attempt; if what
# follows can still match with none left over, the atomic group is
# unremarkable.
pattern (?>a+)b
features atomic-groups
m "aaab" 0 4

# The canonical atomic trap: (?>a+) always consumes every contiguous 'a'
# from wherever it starts, so a mandatory 'a' immediately after it can
# never be satisfied -- not at this start position, and (since a+ is
# greedy and atomic at every possible start position too) not at any
# other start position in this subject either.
pattern (?>a+)a
features atomic-groups
n "aaab"

# a* can match zero characters; on an empty subject that is its only
# possible outcome, atomic or not, and there is still no 'a' left for the
# literal that follows.
pattern (?>a*)a
features atomic-groups
n ""

# An empty atomic group is legal (pcrec's own promise, B.5) and matches
# the empty string.
pattern (?>)
features atomic-groups
m "" 0 0

pattern (?>)a
features atomic-groups
m "a" 0 1

# Nesting: the same commit-and-refuse behaviour applies at every level.
pattern (?>(?>a+)b)
features atomic-groups
m "aab" 0 3
n "aa"

# A LAZY quantifier inside an atomic group still commits to whatever ITS
# OWN first attempt was -- which for a lazy quantifier is the MINIMAL
# match, not the maximal one. (?>a+?) commits to consuming exactly one
# 'a', and cannot expand even though expanding would let a later 'b'
# match. Search is unanchored, so the leftmost position where the
# committed minimal 'a' happens to be followed by 'b' wins -- not
# position 0, where committing to a single 'a' leaves another 'a' (not
# 'b') next.
pattern (?>a+?)b
features atomic-groups
m "aaab" 2 4

pattern (?>a+?)
features atomic-groups
m "aaa" 0 1

# Alternation priority again, worked both ways with real words: the first
# alternative that matches is committed to regardless of length.
pattern (?>cat|category)
features atomic-groups
m "category" 0 3

pattern (?>category|cat)
features atomic-groups
m "category" 0 8
